{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KgsSm9Ge6hice9fkubv1tF","type":"tool_result","content":"14:a world; `Math.random` seeded as in parallel) or **shared world** (one world\n16:`co-op` and `versus` as a shared world. The protocol page now holds only the\n29:**Ghost** (the other players seen in your own world) and a **shared world**\n38:  host's copy runs every shared world, each copy owning its own player.\n40:  game for a new ability. A watcher of a shared world is a copy\n78:   online multiplayer of its own, a shared world. A game declares the one\n115:    creative decisions alone.\n131:| 3 | Watching runs on state, not video | **Kept, without the per-game ability** | Video would need the hub to reach into the game's frame: a capture cannot be handed across frames (tested: `DataCloneError`), so it would tie every game to the hub's origin. It misses the HUDs that most canvas games draw in page elements, and the playing machine pays for the encoding (25–43 ms a 720p frame in software, measured here). State costs a shared world or a race nothing new: a copy with no player of its own draws everything from the snapshots and samples every copy already gets. Parallel shares nothing to draw, so there the live scoreboard is the view, and *Again* keeps everyone playing. What is cut is snapshot, restore and mirror as a per-game ability. |\n132:| 4 | The host is the server, built so a dedicated server can take over later | **Kept: the games are made ready now, the server is built when wanted** | The host's copy runs every shared world today. What multiplies across thousands of games is each game's own part, so that goes in now: a shared world must run when its host is a copy with no player of its own (`AIHub.server`), and `hub-check` plays one such round for every versus and co-op game. Two old rules stay as guidance (the world in plain data apart from drawing; nothing read back from the GPU). Built once, and likely to change before it is needed, so built later: the server program, where it runs, how it joins a session, and the lobby's switch. |\n149:| 0 Watch | Parallel and ghost: the live scoreboard (*Now*). Shared worlds: a copy with no player of its own, fed the same traffic (*Later*) |\n152:| 3 Together | Mode `shared-world`: the host's copy runs the world (`versus` and `co-op` until Nov 2) |\n153:| 4 Natural multiplayer | A shared world, in whatever form the game's own multiplayer takes |\n154:| 5 Couch and remote couch | Couch co-op on one screen stays each game's own (up to four controllers), unchanged. Remote couch co-op is **pinned**: friends far away would need the host's screen streamed to them as video |\n159:parallel and ghost games and the shared worlds, so a dozen shared worlds are\n169:left alone. `Math.random` is seeded by the hub before the game's\n222:   left alone.\n226:   progress and a seeded start expected; and the game on its own compared\n332:  one; an edited copy is reported and left alone. Batch by folder, then spot\n346:  Free plan counts each broadcast once sent and once per receiver: 2 million\n349:  messages a minute today, almost all of it the host's state broadcast every\n355:- **The peer links** (WebRTC data channels) carry game traffic, progress and\n362:  events never does. The protocol now says 15, broadcast once, and keeps a\n377:  a round (about 1,400 games use `localStorage`), and the real saves are\n395:  by side as a short self-contained level, the shared-world patterns with\n398:  agent on its own copy of the game, spend capped, every failure put back);\n414:- Dedicated servers, the part in the games: a shared world must run when its\n431:Then the hub alone, no game changes:\n470:- **Watching** shared worlds and races: a seat with no player of its own,\n477:  (`localStorage['ai-hub:auth']`). Serving the frames from a second hostname\n479:  frames (the splash's pad buttons, Create + Options, the Couch's pad\n498:- A Discord Activity: Discord's proxy carries no WebRTC, so the one network\n504:- Remote couch co-op: friends far away playing a game made for one screen, on\n507:  Couch co-op in one room is the game's own and is unaffected.\n529:- **Up to four players locally, controller first**: welcome. Couch co-op in\n530:  one room is the game's own; remote couch co-op is pinned.\n542:| 1 | Decisions 3 and 4 | Decided Oct 29: state, not video, and no video anywhere (remote couch pinned); dedicated servers prepared in the games now, built when wanted |\n562:  every time. A game-like 720p stream sat at libwebrtc's default 2.0–2.5 Mbps\n574:- **Realtime**: 205 broadcasts in an 80 s, 2-round, 4-player local session\n\n=====INGEST\n\n=====LIBDIRS\nfile\t1081\tlib/three/LICENSE\ndir\t0\tlib/three/addons\nfile\t389591\tlib/three/three.core.js\nfile\t25647\tlib/three/three.tsl.js\nfile\t720999\tlib/three/three.webgpu.js\nfile\t11343\tlib/rapier/LICENSE\nfile\t3091630\tlib/rapier/rapier-simd.mjs\nfile\t2857590\tlib/rapier/rapier.mjs","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9652e092-8cd1-4acd-9328-de6431071aa9","uuid":"192653ab-771e-4852-97bb-292804dfa029","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:48.466Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"14:a world; `Math.random` seeded as in parallel) or **shared world** (one world\n16:`co-op` and `versus` as a shared world. The protocol page now holds only the\n29:**Ghost** (the other players seen in your own world) and a **shared world**\n38:  host's copy runs every shared world, each copy owning its own player.\n40:  game for a new ability. A watcher of a shared world is a copy\n78:   online multiplayer of its own, a shared world. A game declares the one\n115:    creative decisions alone.\n131:| 3 | Watching runs on state, not video | **Kept, without the per-game ability** | Video would need the hub to reach into the game's frame: a capture cannot be handed across frames (tested: `DataCloneError`), so it would tie every game to the hub's origin. It misses the HUDs that most canvas games draw in page elements, and the playing machine pays for the encoding (25–43 ms a 720p frame in software, measured here). State costs a shared world or a race nothing new: a copy with no player of its own draws everything from the snapshots and samples every copy already gets. Parallel shares nothing to draw, so there the live scoreboard is the view, and *Again* keeps everyone playing. What is cut is snapshot, restore and mirror as a per-game ability. |\n132:| 4 | The host is the server, built so a dedicated server can take over later | **Kept: the games are made ready now, the server is built when wanted** | The host's copy runs every shared world today. What multiplies across thousands of games is each game's own part, so that goes in now: a shared world must run when its host is a copy with no player of its own (`AIHub.server`), and `hub-check` plays one such round for every versus and co-op game. Two old rules stay as guidance (the world in plain data apart from drawing; nothing read back from the GPU). Built once, and likely to change before it is needed, so built later: the server program, where it runs, how it joins a session, and the lobby's switch. |\n149:| 0 Watch | Parallel and ghost: the live scoreboard (*Now*). Shared worlds: a copy with no player of its own, fed the same traffic (*Later*) |\n152:| 3 Together | Mode `shared-world`: the host's copy runs the world (`versus` and `co-op` until Nov 2) |\n153:| 4 Natural multiplayer | A shared world, in whatever form the game's own multiplayer takes |\n154:| 5 Couch and remote couch | Couch co-op on one screen stays each game's own (up to four controllers), unchanged. Remote couch co-op is **pinned**: friends far away would need the host's screen streamed to them as video |\n159:parallel and ghost games and the shared worlds, so a dozen shared worlds are\n169:left alone. `Math.random` is seeded by the hub before the game's\n222:   left alone.\n226:   progress and a seeded start expected; and the game on its ow