{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9JymXLZAQmvTcnsA22","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0195BhikYojE6Pxw9jiWd4JE","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/docs/multiplayer-plan.md --jq '.content' | base64 -d > /tmp/.ignore 2>/dev/null; rm -f /tmp/.ignore; gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/docs/multiplayer-plan.md --jq '.content' | base64 -d | sed -n '70,118p;390,430p;525,535p'","description":"Read the hub's multiplayer guidance for shared worlds"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6240,"cache_read_input_tokens":115435,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6240},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9652e092-8cd1-4acd-9328-de6431071aa9","uuid":"d3e47727-827f-4972-9ff4-2b81f841c5e5","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:54.865Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9JyWebipSA7tX4st8c","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0195BhikYojE6Pxw9jiWd4JE":{"command":"gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/docs/multiplayer-plan.md --jq '.content' | base64 -d > /tmp/.ignore 2>/dev/null; rm -f /tmp/.ignore; gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/docs/multiplayer-plan.md --jq '.content' | base64 -d | sed -n '70,118p;390,430p;525,535p'","description":"Read the hub's multiplayer guidance for shared worlds"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0195BhikYojE6Pxw9jiWd4JE","type":"tool_result","content":"1. **Friends, not strangers.** Sessions are for people who invited each other\n   with a link. No matchmaking, no public lobbies, no rankings against\n   strangers.\n2. **No talking in the game.** No chat, voice, emotes, pings or custom names.\n   Friends talk where they already do. The hub shows only the names it\n   generates.\n3. **Every game can be played together, at its honest level.** A game with a\n   score or a finish gets parallel; a game with a hero, ghost; a game with\n   online multiplayer of its own, a shared world. A game declares the one\n   level it plays best, and nothing fakes a level it can't support.\n4. **The hub owns everything around a round; the game owns only the round.**\n   Invite, lobby, seats, names, network, clock, card, scoreboard, results,\n   history: the hub's. A game plays one round and reports how it went.\n5. **Put the intelligence in the hub, keep the game's contract small and\n   stable.** Whatever the hub can do without touching a game (the scoreboard,\n   the deal, *Again*, awards, build battles) it does itself, once.\n   Games do only what only they know: when they are ready, when play starts,\n   their score and their end. That is what makes thousands of games\n   affordable to keep current.\n6. **Every mode, one network.** A game talks only through `AIHub.send` and\n   never opens a connection of its own. Control goes through the session's\n   Supabase channel; anything periodic (game traffic, progress) goes over the\n   players' direct peer-to-peer links.\n7. **State, not video.** Every player runs their own copy, moves their own\n   player instantly and never waits on the network. A watcher runs a copy\n   too, with no player of its own; in parallel mode, the hub's scoreboard is the\n   view.\n8. **Smooth beats strict.** No anti-cheat among friends. Each device moves\n   its own player at once; in head-to-head play, the player who gets hit\n   decides.\n9. **Party pace.** About fifteen seconds from one game's end to the next\n   game's start; nobody watches a loading bar. A round's length is the\n   game's own: the hub calls time at 1.5 × the length a game gives, at most\n   15 minutes, and at 15 minutes when it gives none.\n10. **Every round ranks.** One point for every player beaten, whatever the\n    game, so a session can mix Solitaire and a kart racer.\n11. **A fair start.** Same seed, same rules, same limit; nothing saved on a\n    device carries into a round. In parallel mode, the same seed gives the same\n    start, not the same whole game, and the protocol says so.\n12. **Runs where your friends are.** Any modern browser. WebGPU may make a\n    game look better; a game that truly\n    needs it says so, and the hub deals it only when every player's browser\n    has it.\n13. **Standardize the plumbing, keep the feel.** The hub changes games to\n    standardize them (client, metas, libraries, controls) and leaves their\n    creative decisions alone.\n14. **Additive forever, upgraded by script.** New features only add; a game\n    built for an older client keeps working. Mechanical changes reach every\n    game by a deterministic script, never by a model.\n  has nothing but a touch screen.\n- Fullscreen belongs to the hub in a session: a game's `requestFullscreen()`\n  puts the hub's whole page in fullscreen, so the scoreboard and the cards\n  stay over the game (817 game pages ask for fullscreen).\n- The protocol rewritten as the factory's interface: a one-screen start, side\n  by side as a short self-contained level, the shared-world patterns with\n  measured budgets, a paste-ready self-test, absolute links.\n- Tools: `hub-client.mjs`; `retrofit.mjs` (pick, prepare, check, run: each\n  agent on its own copy of the game, spend capped, every failure put back);\n  `hub-check --retrofit`, `--draws-only` (which knows a WebGPU-only game this\n  browser cannot run), `--record` and `--browser-args`.\n- Migration 0008: a round's mode may be any short word. Applied Oct 29 with\n  0007, then 0009 and 0010, which make every policy say what a guest may read\n  (a guest sees a session only while seated); `supabase/tests/rls.sql` passes\n  live (42 checks), and both advisors show nothing from our migrations.\n  The security advisor's three warnings can't be fixed from the SQL editor:\n  \"Anonymous Sign-Ins Allowed\" on `cron.job` and `cron.job_run_details` is\n  pg_cron's own policy on tables owned by `supabase_admin` (the editor's\n  `postgres` cannot alter them, and guests have no `usage` on schema\n  `cron`), and leaked-password protection is an Auth setting only the Pro\n  plan has.\n- The links a game builder is given are GitHub's (`raw.githubusercontent.com`,\n  the `main` branch), so they outlast any change of host; the site itself\n  stays where it is.\n- Dedicated servers, the part in the games: a shared world must run when its\n  host is a copy with no player of its own, and `hub-check` plays one such\n  round for every versus and co-op game; the round takes a server seat;\n  Star Arena does it. Blaze Cup and Grand Theft Motto III predate the rule:\n  they play as before and get it when servers come.\n- The versus example owns its player, as the protocol says: a key press moves\n  a non-host's player on the next frame (about 17 ms), where it used to wait\n  a median of 420 ms for the host.\n\n**Next**\n\n- **The rest of wave 1**, in batches of about fifty, from the owner's\n  computer. The pilot (*What it costs*) passed 26 of 32 games for $16.96;\n  those 26 are dealt now. First, end a game's Haiku 5.5 tries when one exits\n  without changing anything, and rerun the three games hub-check gave no\n  report on, now that it says why.\n\n- **Attract mode after 10 s idle** on the title screen: in a session there is\n  no title screen, so it never starts there. No conflict.\n- **Testing \"efficient\"**: the agent can still run the harness from the\n  protocol's *Test it*; the hub checks every game at import either way.\n- **Up to four players locally, controller first**: welcome. Couch co-op in\n  one room is the game's own; remote couch co-op is pinned.\n\nIf cc-matrix builds are imported, the hub's importer (`import-matrix-runs.mjs`,\nwritten for gpt-matrix's layout) needs to learn cc-matrix's `runs/<id>/work/`\nlayout: an ai-hub change, made when the first cc-matrix builds come in.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9652e092-8cd1-4acd-9328-de6431071aa9","uuid":"081f57aa-76e6-4b85-8f2d-4e004865a0a2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:56.108Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1. **Friends, not strangers.** Sessions are for people who invited each other\n   with a link. No matchmaking, no public lobbies, no rankings against\n   strangers.\n2. **No talking in the game.** No chat, voice, emotes, pings or custom names.\n   Friends talk where they already do. The hub shows only the names it\n   generates.\n3. **Every game can be played together, at its honest level.** A game with a\n   score or a finish gets parallel; a game with a hero, ghost; a game with\n   online multiplayer of its own, a shared world. A game declares the one\n   level it plays best, and nothing fakes a level it can't support.\n4. **The hub owns everything around a round; the game owns only the round.**\n   Invite, lobby, seats, names, network, clock, card, scoreboard, results,\n   history: the hub's. A game plays one round and reports how it went.\n5. **Put the intelligence in the hub, keep the game's contract small and\n   stable.** Whatever the hub can do without touching a game (the scoreboard,\n   the deal, *Again*, awards, build battles) it does itself, once.\n   Games do only what only they know: when they are ready, when play starts,\n   their score and their end. That is what makes thousands of games\n   affordable to keep current.\n6. **Every mode, one network.** A game talks only through `AIHub.send` and\n   never opens a connection of its own. Control goes through the session's\n   Supabase channel; anything periodic (game traffic, progress) goes over the\n   players' direct peer-to-peer links.\n7. **State, not video.** Every player runs their own copy, moves their own\n   player instantly and never waits on the network. A watcher runs a copy\n   too, with no player of its own; in parallel mode, the hub's scoreboard is the\n   view.\n8. **Smooth beats strict.** No anti-cheat among friends. Each device moves\n   its own player at once; in head-to-head play, the player who gets hit\n   decides.\n9. **Party pace.** About fifteen seconds from one game's end to the next\n   game's start; nobody watches a loading bar. A round's length is the\n   game's own: the hub calls time at 1.5 × the length a game gives, at most\n   15 minutes, and at 15 minutes when it gives none.\n10. **Every round ranks.** One point for every player beaten, whatever the\n    game, so a session can mix Solitaire and a kart racer.\n11. **A fair start.** Same seed, same rules, same limit; nothing saved on a\n    device carries into a round. In parallel mode, the same seed gives the same\n    start, not the same whole game, and the protocol says so.\n12. **Runs where your friends are.** Any modern browser. WebGPU may make a\n    game look better; a game that truly\n    needs it says so, and the hub deals it only when every player's browser\n    has it.\n13. **Standardize the plumbing, keep the feel.** The hub changes games to\n    standardize them (client, metas, libraries, controls) and leaves their\n    creative decisions alone.\n14. **Additive forever, upgraded by script.** New features only add; a game\n    built for an older client keeps working. Mechanical changes reach every\n    game by a deterministic script, never by a model.\n  has nothing but a touch screen.\n- Fullscreen belongs to the hub in a session: a game's `requestFullscreen()`\n  puts the hub's whole page in