{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NfoAsUT2kMWu9qUYqKD5es","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Game Plan: an inked, co-op, isometric action-RPG\n2\t\n3\tYou are building this game alone, from this plan, with no director to ask. TASK.md is the brief and wins any conflict with this plan. Where this plan is silent, decide for yourself using the pillars in §1 and do not stop to ask.\n4\t\n5\t- **Paths** in this plan are relative to your dev folder (the subfolder TASK.md tells you to create). Deliverable names and locations come from your TASK.md.\n6\t- **The title is yours.** Follow TASK.md: silently brainstorm 50 playful riffs on the source's name, then pick one with a real random number generator (run code). Do this first, because your folder and file names depend on it. Keep the title in one constant (`src/data/meta.js`). Nothing in the story, world or code depends on it. A silly title on a grim game is fine: it is a wink on the title screen, and the game never mentions it again.\n7\t- **\"Verified\"** means the planner ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with the pinned versions, in throwaway prototypes. Trust those facts and copy the recipes in Appendix A. Anything else is design intent, so change it if testing shows something better.\n8\t- **Reading order:** §1–§4, then §20 (build order). Use the other sections as reference while you build each part.\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 1. The game in one page\n13\t\n14\t**Genre:** isometric action-RPG (hack-and-slash loot game) with story campaign, endgame runs and 1–4 player co-op. That is what the source is, and the genre is what makes it great.\n15\t**Perspective:** fixed three-quarter overhead camera (perspective lens, 45° yaw, about 55° pitch), so hordes, ground telegraphs and loot all read from a sofa.\n16\t\n17\t**Pitch.** A black bell fell out of a clear sky and split the hill under an abbey. Every toll takes something from the world, and the seventh takes the sun. Four mortals who appear in no holy book, and so belong to neither Heaven nor Hell, fight up through candle-headed dead, a tax-collecting devil lord and the faceless choir above, cutting down hundreds at a time while legendary loot rains out of the dark. It looks like a woodcut in a burnt psalter that has started to move.\n18\t\n19\t**Pillars** (use these to settle any decision the plan leaves open):\n20\t1. **Every press kills something, and it feels wonderful.** Impact within 0.2 s of the button. Hit-stop, flash, ink spatter, ragdolls, breaking furniture, rumble, a thump you feel. Enemies come in crowds and die in crowds.\n21\t2. **The next drop might change everything.** Loot arrives constantly and has a moment: colour, beam, bell, big name. Legendaries change how a skill works in a way you can see.\n22\t3. **Build freely, never be punished.** Any skill in any slot, swap out of combat at no cost, respec everything.\n23\t4. **The world is dying loudly.** Grand, doom-laden melodrama. Villains taunt you personally over the whole level. Lore is read aloud while you keep fighting. Gallows humour at the edges.\n24\t5. **Made for a sofa.** One controller or four, on a TV at ten feet. Large text, few words, one thing on screen at a time. Keyboard and mouse get the same screens and feel native.\n25\t\n26\t**Length.** A first campaign run takes 50–70 minutes. After it, *The Hours* (endgame runs) can be replayed freely. A hub session round takes about 10 minutes.\n27\t\n28\t**Slice, not sprawl.** The demo is one complete game loop built to final quality on infrastructure that can scale. Three short acts show every mechanic. Content lives in data tables so designers can add more without touching systems (§4.3).\n29\t\n30\t**No stubs, ever.** If you run short of capacity, follow the cut rules in §20. Removing something cleanly is allowed. Shipping a stub is not.\n31\t\n32\t---\n33\t\n34\t## 2. What we take from the source, and what we never take\n35\t\n36\tThis is an **original game inspired by the source**. Take the genre, the feel and the kinds of systems. Never take its names, characters, art, music, levels or look.\n37\t\n38\t| What makes the source distinctive | Our version |\n39\t|---|---|\n40\t| Fallen star, doomed town, cathedral with dead beneath it | The Black Bell falls on the Abbey of Saint Oren above Tallowgate |\n41\t| Heaven vs Hell with mortals caught between, heroes outside the order | The Choir Immaculate vs the Under-Court, heroes are \"the Unsung\", written in neither book |\n42\t| Five-act melodrama with taunting villains | Three canonical-hour acts: Vespers, Compline, Matins. Villains who never shut up |\n43\t| Lore books read aloud while you play | Margin Pages, narrated by Archivist Odalys over the fighting |\n44\t| Class resources with different rhythms | Contrition, Wick (with Overburn), Dirge, Oil + Nerve |\n45\t| Active skills with rune modifiers, passives, free respec | Skills with Glyphs, passives, free swap out of combat |\n46\t| Rarity colours, affixes, legendary powers, sets | Worn → Common → Blessed → Exalted → Legendary / Set |\n47\t| Salvage, crafting, enchanting, gems, the legendary-power cube | Hesper's forge, Nan Tallow's wax-work, the Reliquary |\n48\t| Elite packs with random affixes, boss phases, ground telegraphs | Champion and Exalted packs, 10 elite vows, 5 multi-phase bosses |\n49\t| Treasure thief who flees with loot | The Tithe Imp (\"Arrears! Arrears!\") |\n50\t| Grotesque butcher mini-boss | The Chandler (\"MORE WICKS!\") |\n51\t| Kill-streak XP bonus, destructible scenery, physics corpses | CARNAGE ×37, Ruinous, Devastation, Rapier ragdolls and debris |\n52\t| Procedural zones, events, shrines, health globes, town portal, waypoints | Stamp-built zones, Vigils and Cursed Reliquaries, Saint's relic shrines, Heartsparks, Homeward Candle, Vesper Shrines |\n53\t| Timed randomized endgame runs, scaling difficulty tiers | The Hours, the timed Vigil Trial, Pilgrim → Penance III |\n54\t| Couch co-op, stick movement, evade roll | All kept, designed first for the pad |\n55\t\n56\t**Never use**, in names, text, art or sound: Diablo (except the About/Credits \"Source\" field, the exit interview's source and series fields, and the title riff if you use it), Sanctuary, Tristram, Cain, Leah, Adria, Tyrael, Azmodan, Belial, Maghda, Nephalem, Horadrim, Barbarian, Demon Hunter, Witch Doctor, Crusader, Necromancer, Wizard, Monk (as classes), Treasure Goblin, Whimsyshire, Nephalem Rift, Greater Rift, Paragon, Kanai, Fury, Hatred, Discipline, Arcane Power, its logo lettering, its round-globe HUD, its class art, its music or any melody resembling it. Do not copy its level layouts. Every in-game name comes from this plan's bible or your own invention in its spirit. All text you write is original.\n57\t\n58\t---\n59\t\n60\t## 3. Verified technical facts\n61\t\n62\tThese cost real time to discover. Do not rediscover them.\n63\t\n64\t### 3.1 Stack (pinned, verified)\n65\t\n66\t| Piece | Choice |\n67\t|---|---|\n68\t| Renderer | `three@0.180.0`, `import * as THREE from 'three/webgpu'`, TSL from `three/tsl`, addons from `three/addons/...` |\n69\t| Physics | `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` (WASM inlined as base64, works in the browser and in Node) |\n70\t| Language/build | Plain ES modules, `esbuild` (installed) bundling to a single inline IIFE |\n71\t| UI | DOM + CSS overlay above the canvas, never canvas text |\n72\t| Audio | Web Audio only, everything synthesized |\n73\t| Input | Gamepad API (standard mapping), keyboard, mouse |\n74\t| Fonts | `@fontsource/uncial-antiqua` (titles, 20 KB) and `@fontsource/alegreya` 500/800 (all UI text, 24 KB each), OFL, inlined as base64 woff2 from each package's `files/*-latin-*-normal.woff2`. Credit them in About/Credits. These subsets have no symbols like ▲▼◆★, so draw symbols as inline SVG |\n75\t| Packaging | One HTML file, no external requests. TASK.md allows CDNs, but inline is simpler and verified on `file://` (3.2 MB for the renderer, Rapier and a test scene) |\n76\t\n77\tThe hub repo also has a `lib/kit` built on a newer three.js. Do not use it: TASK.md pins R180.\n78\t\n79\t### 3.2 WebGPU in this environment\n80\t\n81\t| Launch flags for Playwright Chromium | Result (verified) |\n82\t|---|---|\n83\t| `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` (the list in TASK.md) | **Broken: the device is destroyed right after the first frame.** Later `render()` calls silently do nothing, so frame rates look great and every screenshot is blank |\n84\t| Add `--use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader` | **Use this.** WebGPU stays alive, and `page.screenshot()` and `canvas.toDataURL()` show real pixels, so TASK.md's warning about blank screenshots no longer applies (`--use-angle=swiftshader` is the part that matters) |\n85\t| No flags | No adapter. `WebGPURenderer` falls back to its WebGL2 backend by itself. TSL materials and the ink post chain all work, screenshots work |\n86\t\n87\t- **Always assert the backend** in browser tests (`renderer.backend.isWebGPUBackend`) and listen for `device.lost`. A dead device looks like a fast game.\n88\t- WebKit runs the WebGL2 path (verified, screenshots work). Firefox has no `navigator.gpu`, and Playwright's Firefox gave `getContext('webgl2') === null` on real pages in the planner's tests (plain `about:blank` worked), which made three throw during `renderer.init()`. So: wrap renderer init in try/catch, and if no backend starts, show a plain readable message instead of an uncaught error. Do not spend time on Firefox beyond that.\n89\t- Software frame rates at 1280×720 with a heavy scene (60 skinned figures, 17 lights, shadows, ink + bloom): about 2–5 fps on WebGPU, 2 fps on Chromium WebGL2, 4 fps on WebKit. **Never make a test depend on wall-clock frame rate.** Tests drive the sim with `step(n)` (§19).\n90\t- TASK.md's rule stands: both `file://` and `http://127.0.0.1` count as secure. `localStorage`, `navigator.clipboard`, `RTCPeerConnection` all exist on `file://` (verified).\n91\t\n92\t### 3.3 What rebuilds GPU pipelines in r180 (verified by counting pipeline creations)\n93\t\n94\t| Change | New pipelines | Rule |\n95\t|---|---|---|\n96\t| Set a light's `intensity` to 0 | 0 | **Pool lights: fixed count, move them, dim them** |\n97\t| `light.visible = false`, or adding a light | Rebuilds every lit pipeline | Never at runtime |\n98\t| Toggle a light's `castShadow` | Rebuilds every lit pipeline | Decide at load and never change |\n99\t| Toggle `material.transparent` | 1–2 | Create both variants up front |\n100\t| New mesh or clone with an existing material | 0 | Fine anytime |\n101\t| New material with the same structure (same nodes, different colour) | 0 | **Each monster may own its material** |\n102\t| Skinned clone (`SkeletonUtils.clone`), shared or own material | 0 | Spawn crowds freely |\n103\t| Uniform change (`flash.value = 1`) | 0 | Do per-object effects with uniforms |\n104\t| Each new `InstancedMesh`, even with the same material | **1 each** | Create instanced batches at zone load and keep their number small. Merge static level geometry instead (`mergeGeometries`) |\n105\t\n106\tTwo traps that give **no error**, only a wrong picture (both verified on both backends):\n107\t- **`renderer.compileAsync(scene, camera)` before the post chain makes the scene pass draw nothing but the background.** Do not call it. To avoid first-use stalls, build the zone behind the loading veil with one instance of every FX material in view, and render one real frame with `post.render()`.\n108\t- **Inside a post-processing `Fn`, `cameraNear`, `cameraFar`, `cameraProjectionMatrixInverse` and `cameraWorldMatrix` are the full-screen quad's camera, not your scene camera.** Bind uniforms to the scene camera instead, as in A.4. Checked numerically: the reconstructed world position then matches a CPU ray cast to within 0.03 u and stays correct when the camera pans.\n109\t\n110\tOther r180 facts: `MeshToonNodeMaterial` with a `DataTexture` gradient works. `material.emissiveNode` and `material.maskNode` work on both backends (recipes in Appendix A). `interleavedGradientNoise` is **not** exported from `three/tsl` in r180. `PostProcessing` is the class name in r180 (later releases rename it, so ignore newer docs). The hub's notes for a newer three.js say WebGPU draws `Points` at 1 px and does not support `LineLoop`. The planner did not re-test those on r180, so use instanced quads for particles and avoid `LineLoop`.\n111\t\n112\t### 3.4 Physics\n113\t\n114\t`await RAPIER.init()` logs a \"deprecated parameters\" console warning. It is harmless. Verified working: `createCharacterController`, `computeColliderMovement`, `castRay`, kinematic bodies, dynamic bodies. **150 character-controller actors against 400 static colliders cost 2.4 ms per step in Node.** Use the controller for every actor.\n115\t\n116\t### 3.5 AI Hub (verified against the real harness with a prototype: all pass at 2, 3, 4 players, host-leave, server, on both backends)\n117\t\n118\tFull details in §18. The traps:\n119\t- `session.js` must sit **byte-identical** between its banner and last line, exactly once, as the first script in `<head>` right after `<meta charset>`. The hub hashes it from disk. So paste it into the HTML template outside the bundle, and make sure the text `/*! aihub-session ` occurs nowhere else in the file.\n120\t- Meta tags must be written `name` first, then `content`, double quotes, no quote characters inside (the checker uses a regex).\n121\t- `AIHub.connect()` must be the first thing your main script does, and `AIHub.ready()` must follow as soon as the **sim** is ready, never waiting for the renderer. The harness gives 10 s for hello and 20 s for ready, and all copies share one main thread.\n122\t- **`window.__hubCheck.pass` can be true with a broken game.** If your game fails right after `ready()`, the harness stops early with only a few checks, all passing. Require `checks.length >= 13` too (14 with your own card, verified).\n123\t- Any console line starting `[AIHub]` fails the hub's check. That includes sends over the rate limit and oversized sends. Use the outbox in §18.4.\n124\t- `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` buffers its \"port\" line when stdout is a pipe, and a script reading it **hangs forever**. Use `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`.\n125\t- With a heavy scene on software WebGL and four copies, the shared main thread gives each copy about 1.5 fps and the sim timer about 2 Hz. That still passes (verified) as long as the sim catches up from elapsed time and time-up is handled as an event. Add the render governor (§18.6) so a heavier scene stays safe.\n126\t\n127\t### 3.6 Serverless online (verified on `file://`)\n128\t\n129\tWebRTC with copy-paste invite codes connects two pages in the same browser, with both same-context and separate-context pages, on both a reliable ordered channel and an unordered no-retransmit channel. Codes are about 860 characters without STUN. With a public STUN server, the offer grew to about 1,050 characters, gathering ran into the 3 s cap, and the pages still connected. BroadcastChannel only reaches pages in the same context, so do not build on it.\n130\t\n131\t### 3.7 Touch-only detection (verified on iPhone, iPad and Pixel profiles against desktop)\n132\t\n133\t`navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0 && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`. Touch-only until a gamepad connects or a key is pressed.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## 4. Architecture\n138\t\n139\t### 4.1 Layout\n140\t\n141\t```\n142\tpackage.json              pinned deps; no other runtime deps\n143\ttools/build.mjs           esbuild + HTML assembly -> dist/game.html; checks the client hash\n144\ttools/test.mjs            the one test command (§19)\n145\ttools/hub_run.py          drives the hub harness (§19)\n146\ttools/make-exit.mjs       writes the exit-interview JSON from src/data/about.js and validates it\n147\tvendor/hub/               session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, verbatim from the hub repo\n148\tsrc/index.template.html   head tags, CSS, pasted client, bundle slot\n149\tsrc/main.js               boot order (§18.1)\n150\tsrc/core/                 rng, clock, events, math, save, settings, tuning"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9652e092-8cd1-4acd-9328-de6431071aa9","uuid":"7bb7d36c-f12e-4524-ad09-5a2eca359509","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:27:33.592Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211950__xd6e5e2/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Game Plan: an inked, co-op, isometric action-RPG\n\nYou are building this game alone, from this plan, with no director to ask. TASK.md is the brief and wins any conflict with this plan. Where this plan is silent, decide for yourself using the pillars in §1 and do not stop to ask.\n\n- **Paths** in this plan are relative to your dev folder (the subfolder TASK.md tells you to create). Deliverable names and locations come from your TASK.md.\n- **The title is yours.** Follow TASK.md: silently brainstorm 50 playful riffs on the source's name, then pick one with a real random number generator (run code). Do this first, because your folder and file names depend on it. Keep the title in one constant (`src/data/meta.js`). Nothing in the story, world or code depends on it. A silly title on a grim game is fine: it is a wink on the title screen, and the game never mentions it again.\n- **\"Verified\"** means the planner ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with the pinned versions, in throwaway prototypes. Trust those facts and copy the recipes in Appendix A. Anything else is design intent, so change it if testing shows something better.\n- **Reading order:** §1–§4, then §20 (build order). Use the other sections as reference while you build each part.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game in one page\n\n**Genre:** isometric action-RPG (hack-and-slash loot game) with story campaign, endgame runs and 1–4 player co-op. That is what the source is, and the genre is what makes it great.\n**Perspective:** fixed three-quarter overhead camera (perspective lens, 45° yaw, about 55° pitch), so hordes, ground telegraphs and loot all read from a sofa.\n\n**Pitch.** A black bell fell out of a clear sky and split the hill under an abbey. Every toll takes something from the world, and the seventh takes the sun. Four mortals who appear in no holy book, and so belong to neither Heaven nor Hell, fight up through candle-headed dead, a tax-collecting devil lord and the faceless choir above, cutting down hundreds at a time while legendary loot rains out of the dark. It looks like a woodcut in a burnt psalter that has started to move.\n\n**Pillars** (use these to settle any decision the plan leaves open):\n1. **Every press kills something, and it feels wonderful.** Impact within 0.2 s of the button. Hit-stop, flash, ink spatter, ragdolls, breaking furniture, rumble, a thump you feel. Enemies come in crowds and die in crowds.\n2. **The next drop might change everything.** Loot arrives constantly and has a moment: colour, beam, bell, big name. Legendaries change how a skill works in a way you can see.\n3. **Build freely, never be punished.** Any skill in any slot, swap out of combat at no cost, respec everything.\n4. **The world is dying loudly.** Grand, doom-laden melodrama. Villains taunt you personally over the whole level. Lore is read aloud while you keep fighting. Gallows humour at the edges.\n5. **Made for a sofa.** One controller or four, on a TV at ten feet. Large text, few words, one thing on screen at a time. Keyboard and mouse get the same screens and feel native.\n\n**Length.** A first campaign run takes 50–70 minutes. After it, *The Hours* (endgame runs) can be replayed freely. A hub session round takes about 10 minutes.\n\n**Slice, not sprawl.** The demo is one complete game loop built to final quality on infrastructure that can scale. Three short acts show every mechanic. Content lives in data tables so designers can add more without touching systems (§4.3).\n\n**No stubs, ever.** If you run short of capacity, follow the cut rules in §20. Removing something cleanly is allowed. Shipping a stub is not.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What we take from the source, and what we never take\n\nThis is an **original game inspired by the source**. Take the genre, the feel and the kinds of systems. Never take its names, characters, art, music, levels or look.\n\n| What makes the source distinctive | Our version |\n|---|---|\n| Fallen star, doomed town, cathedral with dead beneath it | The Black Bell falls on the Abbey of Saint Oren above Tallowgate |\n| Heaven vs Hell with mortals caught between, heroes outside the order | The Choir Immaculate vs the Under-Court, heroes are \"the Unsung\", written in neither book |\n| Five-act melodrama with taunting villains | Three canonical-hour acts: Vespers, Compline, Matins. Villains who never shut up |\n| Lore books read aloud while you play | Margin Pages, narrated by Archivist Odalys over the fighting |\n| Class resources with different rhythms | Contrition, Wick (with Overburn), Dirge, Oil + Nerve |\n| Active skills with rune modifiers, passives, free respec | Skills with Glyphs, passives, free swap out of combat |\n| Rarity colours, affixes, legendary powers, sets | Worn → Common → Blessed → Exalted → Legendary / Set |\n| Salvage, crafting, enchanting, gems, the legendary-power cube | Hesper's forge, Nan Tallow's wax-work, the Reliquary |\n| Elite packs with random affixes, boss phases, ground telegraphs | Champion and Exalted packs, 10 elite vows, 5 multi-phase bosses |\n| Treasure thief who flees with loot | The Tithe Imp (\"Arrears! Arrears!\") |\n| Grotesque butcher mini-boss | The Chandler (\"MORE WICKS!\") |\n| Kill-streak XP bonus, destructible scenery, physics corpses | CARNAGE ×37, Ruinous, Devastation, Rapier ragdolls and debris |\n| Procedural zones, events, shrines, health globes, town portal, waypoints | Stamp-built zones, Vigils and Cursed Reliquaries, Saint's relic shrines, Heartsparks, Homeward Candle, Vesper Shrines |\n| Timed randomized endgame runs, scaling difficulty tiers | The Hours, the timed Vigil Trial, Pilgrim → Penance III |\n| Couch co-op, stick movement, evade roll | All kept, designed first for the pad |\n\n**Never use**, in names, text, art or sound: Diablo (except the About/Credits \"Source\" field, the exit interview's source and series fields, and the title riff if you use it), Sanctuary, Tristram, Cain, Leah, Adria, Tyrael, Azmodan, Belial, Maghda, Nephalem, Horadrim, Barbarian, Demon Hunter, Witch Doctor, Crusader, Necromancer, Wizard, Monk (as classes), Treasure Goblin, Whimsyshire, Nephalem Rift, Greater Rift, Paragon, Kanai, Fury, Hatred, Discipline, Arcane Power, its logo lettering, its round-globe HUD, its class art, its music or any melody resembling it. Do not copy its level layouts. Every in-game name comes from this plan's bible or your own invention in its spirit. All text you write is original.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Verified technical facts\n\nThese cost real time to discover. Do not rediscover them.\n\n### 3.1 Stack (pinned, verified)\n\n| Piece | Choice |\n|---|---|\n| Renderer | `three@0.180.0`, `import * as THREE from 'three/webgpu'`, TSL from `three/tsl`, addons from `three/addons/...` |\n| Physics | `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` (WASM inlined as base64, works in the browser and in Node) |\n| Language/build | Plain ES modules, `esbuild` (installed) bundling to a single inline IIFE |\n| UI | DOM + CSS overlay above the canvas, never canvas text |\n| Audio | Web Audio only, everything synthesized |\n| Input | Gamepad API (standard mapping), keyboard, mouse |\n| Fonts | `@fontsource/uncial-antiqua` (titles, 20 KB) and `@fontsource/alegreya` 500/800 (all UI text, 24 KB each), OFL, inlined as base64 woff2 from each package's `files/*-latin-*-normal.woff2`. Credit them in About/Credits. These subsets have no symbols like ▲▼◆★, so draw symbols as inline SVG |\n| Packaging | One HTML file, no external requests. TASK.md allows CDNs, but inline is simpler and verified on `file://` (3.2 MB for the renderer, Rapier and a test scene) |\n\nThe hub repo also has a `lib/kit` built on a newer three.js. Do not use it: TASK.md pins R180.\n\n### 3.2 WebGPU in this environment\n\n| Launch flags for Playwright Chromium | Result (verified) |\n|---|---|\n| `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` (the list in TASK.md) | **Broken: the device is destroyed right after the first frame.** Later `render()` calls silently do nothing, so frame rates look great and every screenshot is blank |\n| Add `--use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader` | **Use this.** WebGPU stays alive, and `page.screenshot()` and `canvas.toDataURL()` show real pixels, so TASK.md's warning about blank screenshots no longer applies (`--use-angle=swiftshader` is the part that matters) |\n| No flags | No adapter. `WebGPURenderer` falls back to its WebGL2 backend by itself. TSL materials and the ink post chain all work, screenshots work |\n\n- **Always assert the backend** in browser tests (`renderer.backend.isWebGPUBackend`) and listen for `device.lost`. A dead device looks like a fast game.\n- WebKit runs the WebGL2 path (verified, screenshots work). Firefox has no `navigator.gpu`, and Playwright's Firefox gave `getContext('webgl2') === null` on real pages in the planner's tests (plain `about:blank` worked), which made three throw during `renderer.init()`. So: wrap renderer init in try/catch, and if no backend starts, show a plain readable message instead of an uncaught error. Do not spend time on Firefox beyond that.\n- Software frame rates at 1280×720 with a heavy scene (60 skinned figures, 17 lights, shadows, ink + bloom): about 2–5 fps on WebGPU, 2 fps on Chromium WebGL2, 4 fps on WebKit. **Never make a test depend on wall-clock frame rate.** Tests drive the sim with `step(n)` (§19).\n- TASK.md's rule stands: both `file://` and `http://127.0.0.1` count as secure. `localStorage`, `navigator.clipboard`, `RTCPeerConnection` all exist on `file://` (verified).\n\n### 3.3 What rebuilds GPU pipelines in r180 (verified by counting pipeline creations)\n\n| Change | New pipelines | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Set a light's `intensity` to 0 | 0 | **Pool lights: fixed count, move them, dim them** |\n| `light.visible = false`, or adding a light | Rebuilds every lit pipeline | Never at runtime |\n| Toggle a light's `castShadow` | Rebuilds every lit pipeline | Decide at load and never change |\n| Toggle `material.transparent` | 1–2 | Create both variants up front |\n| New mesh or clone with an existing material | 0 | Fine anytime |\n| New material with the same structure (same nodes, different colour) | 0 | **Each monster may own its material** |\n| Skinned clone (`SkeletonUtils.clone`), shared or own material | 0 | Spawn crowds freely |\n| Uniform change (`flash.value = 1`) | 0 | Do per-object effects with uniforms |\n| Each new `InstancedMesh`, even with the same material | **1 each** | Create instanced batches at zone load and keep their number small. Merge static level geometry instead (`mergeGeometries`) |\n\nTwo traps that give **no error**, only a wrong picture (both verified on both backends):\n- **`renderer.compileAsync(scene, camera)` before the post chain makes the scene pass draw nothing but the background.** Do not call it. To avoid first-use stalls, build the zone behind the loading veil with one instance of every FX material in view, and render one real frame with `post.render()`.\n- **Inside a post-processing `Fn`, `cameraNear`, `cameraFar`, `cameraProjectionMatrixInverse` and `cameraWorldMatrix` are the full-screen quad's camera, not your scene camera.** Bind uniforms to the scene camera instead, as in A.4. Checked numerically: the reconstructed world position then matches a CPU ray cast to within 0.03 u and stays correct when the camera pans.\n\nOther r180 facts: `MeshToonNodeMaterial` with a `DataTexture` gradient works. `material.emissiveNode` and `material.maskNode` work on both backends (recipes in Appendix A). `interleavedGradientNoise` is **not** exported from `three/tsl` in r180. `PostProcessing` is the class name in r180 (later releases rename it, so ignore newer docs). The hub's notes for a newer three.js say WebGPU draws `Points` at 1 px and does not support `LineLoop`. The planner did not re-test those on r180, so use instanced quads for particles and avoid `LineLoop`.\n\n### 3.4 Physics\n\n`await RAPIER.init()` logs a \"deprecated parameters\" console warning. It is harmless. Verified working: `createCharacterController`, `computeColliderMovement`, `castRay`, kinematic bodies, dynamic bodies. **150 character-controller actors against 400 static colliders cost 2.4 ms per step in Node.** Use the controller for every actor.\n\n### 3.5 AI Hub (verified against the real harness with a prototype: all pass at 2, 3, 4 players, host-leave, server, on both backends)\n\nFull details in §18. The traps:\n- `session.js` must sit **byte-identical** between its banner and last line, exactly once, as the first script in `<head>` right after `<meta charset>`. The hub hashes it from disk. So paste it into the HTML template outside the bundle, and make sure the text `/*! aihub-session ` occurs nowhere else in the file.\n- Meta tags must be written `name` first, then `content`, double quotes, no quote characters inside (the checker uses a regex).\n- `AIHub.connect()` must be the first thing your main script does, and `AIHub.ready()` must follow as soon as the **sim** is ready, never waiting for the renderer. The harness gives 10 s for hello and 20 s for ready, and all copies share one main thread.\n- **`window.__hubCheck.pass` can be true with a broken game.** If your game fails right after `ready()`, the harness stops early with only a few checks, all passing. Require `checks.length >= 13` too (14 with your own card, verified).\n- Any console line starting `[AIHub]` fails the hub's check. That includes sends over the rate limit and oversized sends. Use the outbox in §18.4.\n- `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` buffers its \"port\" line when stdout is a pipe, and a script reading it **hangs forever**. Use `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`.\n- With a heavy scene on software WebGL and four copies, the shared main thread gives each copy about 1.5 fps and the sim timer about 2 Hz. That still passes (verified) as long as the sim catches up from elapsed time and time-up is handled as an event. Add the render governor (§18.6) so a heavier scene stays safe.\n\n### 3.6 Serverless online (verified on `file://`)\n\nWebRTC with copy-paste invite codes connects two pages in the same browser, with both same-context and separate-context pages, on both a reliable ordered channel and an unordered no-retransmit channel. Codes are about 860 characters without STUN. With a public STUN server, the offer grew to about 1,050 characters, gathering ran into the 3 s cap, and the pages still connected. BroadcastChannel only reaches pages in the same context, so do not build on it.\n\n### 3.7 Touch-only detection (verified on iPhone, iPad and Pixel profiles against desktop)\n\n`navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0 && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`. Touch-only until a gamepad connects or a key is pressed.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Layout\n\n```\npackage.json              pinned deps; no other runtime deps\ntools/build.mjs           esbuild + HTML assembly -> dist/game.html; checks the client hash\ntools/test.mjs            the one test command (§19)\ntools/hub_run.py          drives the hub harness (§19)\ntools/make-exit.mjs       writes the exit-interview JSON from src/data/about.js and validates it\nvendor/hub/               session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, verbatim from the hub repo\nsrc/index.template.html   head tags, CSS, pasted client, bundle slot\nsrc/main.js               boot order (§18.1)\nsrc/core/                 rng, clock, events, math, save, settings, tuning","numLines":150,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1267}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NfoAsUT2kMWu9qUYqKD5es","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
