{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjED41BL4xP3oErMfSaQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011CwMA67L7fyLeZx3TMDeAH","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np=open('PLAN.md').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global p\n    assert a in p, a[:70]\n    p=p.replace(a,b,1)\nrep(\"| Wheel | Camera zoom in the world, scroll in menus |\",\"| Mouse wheel | Camera zoom in the world, scroll in menus |\")\nrep(\"`name` is your title.\",\"`name` is your title. Suggested tagline: `Seven tolls, and the sun forgets the way`. Suggested description: `An inked co-op action-RPG: cut through candle-headed dead and a tax-collecting devil lord to silence a black bell before the seventh toll.` Use the same tagline in the page `<title>`.\")\nrep(\"| Testing: headless, whole-game script, one quiet command, no review subagents | §19 |\",\"| Testing: headless, whole-game script, one quiet command, no review subagents | §19 |\\n| Effort: yours to spend | Spend it where the pillars in §1 say it shows |\")\nopen('PLAN.md','w').write(p)\nEOF\ncat > planner-exit-interview.json <<'EOF'\n[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"The AI Hub integration is concrete and testable. A protocol page, a real harness and a real checker meant I could prove the multiplayer design against the actual checks rather than guess at it. The 'This computer' section also helped a lot, because exact versions and tools let me run the pinned stack before writing anything down. And the split of decisions is clear: I own genre, perspective and design, each builder owns its title and file names.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Three things. First, 'multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the hub's docs only say a game is unaffected outside a session, so it isn't clear whether standalone play needs its own multiplayer. I planned couch co-op plus WebRTC invite codes to cover the stronger reading. Second, the engine wording ('native style', 'asset library', 'native animation system', 'headless tools') suits a real engine, but three.js is a library with no asset library or headless tools, so I had to interpret each phrase. Third, the WebGPU flags: the listed set looks correct, but on this machine the device is destroyed right after the first frame, and the note that a WebGPU canvas 'can come out blank' makes that failure look expected.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Scoping a feature-complete action-RPG for builders whose capacity I don't know. Every system has to be real with no stubs, so the plan has to be ambitious and also say what to cut, and in what order, if a builder runs short. Keeping the plan trustworthy was the other hard part: two graphics recipes failed silently and I only caught them by running the plan's code exactly as written. compileAsync before the post chain drew an empty scene, and camera nodes inside a post pass read the wrong camera. Capturing the source's feel with an entirely original world, cast and look also took care.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Correct the WebGPU flags by adding --use-angle=swiftshader. Add -u to the http.server tip, because when a script reads the port from a pipe the line is buffered and the script hangs (it cost me about 17 minutes). State plainly whether multiplayer outside the hub is required and in what form. Tell the planner which models and effort levels will build from the plan, and how long a demo should run, so the scope can be sized to fit. Use library-neutral wording instead of 'engine' when the stack is three.js. Mention that the harness's pass flag can be true after an early exit with only a few checks, so the check count matters too.\"\n  }\n]\nEOF\npython3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('planner-exit-interview.json'))\nqs=['What did you like most about this prompt?','What was confusing?','What was the hardest thing to do?','What would you change in this prompt?']\nassert [x['question'] for x in d]==qs and all(set(x)=={'question','answer'} and x['answer'].strip() for x in d)\nprint('exit interview valid,',len(d),'answers')\"\necho '--- my leftover servers (cwd inside my scratch folder only):'\nfor pid in $(pgrep -f \"http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1\"); do c=$(readlink /proc/$pid/cwd 2>/dev/null); case \"$c\" in \"$PWD\"*) echo \"$pid $c\"; kill \"$pid\";; esac; done\necho '--- my leftover browsers (cwd inside my scratch folder only):'\nfor pid in $(pgrep -f \"chrome|firefox|WebKit|pw_run\"); do c=$(readlink /proc/$pid/cwd 2>/dev/null); case \"$c\" in \"$PWD\"*) echo \"$pid $c\";; esac; done\ndu -sh .spike; rm -rf .spike; ls -la; git status --short . | head -10; grep -n -i -E \"exit-interview\\.json|GameTitle|claude-opus|check-environment|\\.spike\" PLAN.md | head; wc -w PLAN.md","description":"Write and validate exit interview, remove scratch folder, stop my leftovers"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9777,"cache_read_input_tokens":393030,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9777},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9652e092-8cd1-4acd-9328-de6431071aa9","uuid":"e0de7c4e-9326-422e-be3c-7fce94328f15","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:28:43.625Z","request_id":"req_011CgjED3hKmLevyDz2VXyw1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011CwMA67L7fyLeZx3TMDeAH":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np=open('PLAN.md').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global p\n    assert a in p, a[:70]\n    p=p.replace(a,b,1)\nrep(\"| Wheel | Camera zoom in the world, scroll in menus |\",\"| Mouse wheel | Camera zoom in the world, scroll in menus |\")\nrep(\"`name` is your title.\",\"`name` is your title. Suggested tagline: `Seven tolls, and the sun forgets the way`. Suggested description: `An inked co-op action-RPG: cut through candle-headed dead and a tax-collecting devil lord to silence a black bell before the seventh toll.` Use the same tagline in the page `<title>`.\")\nrep(\"| Testing: headless, whole-game script, one quiet command, no review subagents | §19 |\",\"| Testing: headless, whole-game script, one quiet command, no review subagents | §19 |\\n| Effort: yours to spend | Spend it where the pillars in §1 say it shows |\")\nopen('PLAN.md','w').write(p)\nEOF\ncat > planner-exit-interview.json <<'EOF'\n[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"The AI Hub integration is concrete and testable. A protocol page, a real harness and a real checker meant I could prove the multiplayer design against the actual checks rather than guess at it. The 'This computer' section also helped a lot, because exact versions and tools let me run the pinned stack before writing anything down. And the split of decisions is clear: I own genre, perspective and design, each builder owns its title and file names.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Three things. First, 'multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the hub's docs only say a game is unaffected outside a session, so it isn't clear whether standalone play needs its own multiplayer. I planned couch co-op plus WebRTC invite codes to cover the stronger reading. Second, the engine wording ('native style', 'asset library', 'native animation system', 'headless tools') suits a real engine, but three.js is a library with no asset library or headless tools, so I had to interpret each phrase. Third, the WebGPU flags: the listed set looks correct, but on this machine the device is destroyed right after the first frame, and the note that a WebGPU canvas 'can come out blank' makes that failure look expected.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Scoping a feature-complete action-RPG for builders whose capacity I don't know. Every system has to be real with no stubs, so the plan has to be ambitious and also say what to cut, and in what order, if a builder runs short. Keeping the plan trustworthy was the other hard part: two graphics recipes failed silently and I only caught them by running the plan's code exactly as written. compileAsync before the post chain drew an empty scene, and camera nodes inside a post pass read the wrong camera. Capturing the source's feel with an entirely original world, cast and look also took care.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Correct the WebGPU flags by adding --use-angle=swiftshader. Add -u to the http.server tip, because when a script reads the port from a pipe the line is buffered and the script hangs (it cost me about 17 minutes). State plainly whether multiplayer outside the hub is required and in what form. Tell the planner which models and effort levels will build from the plan, and how long a demo should run, so the scope can be sized to fit. Use library-neutral wording instead of 'engine' when the stack is three.js. Mention that the harness's pass flag can be true after an early exit with only a few checks, so the check count matters too.\"\n  }\n]\nEOF\npython3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('planner-exit-interview.json'))\nqs=['What did you like most about this prompt?','What was confusing?','What was the hardest thing to do?','What would you change in this prompt?']\nassert [x['question'] for x in d]==qs and all(set(x)=={'question','answer'} and x['answer'].strip() for x in d)\nprint('exit interview valid,',len(d),'answers')\"\necho '--- my leftover servers (cwd inside my scratch folder only):'\nfor pid in $(pgrep -f \"http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1\"); do c=$(readlink /proc/$pid/cwd 2>/dev/null); case \"$c\" in \"$PWD\"*) echo \"$pid $c\"; kill \"$pid\";; esac; done\necho '--- my leftover browsers (cwd inside my scratch folder only):'\nfor pid in $(pgrep -f \"chrome|firefox|WebKit|pw_run\"); do c=$(readlink /proc/$pid/cwd 2>/dev/null); case \"$c\" in \"$PWD\"*) echo \"$pid $c\";; esac; done\ndu -sh .spike; rm -rf .spike; ls -la; git status --short . | head -10; grep -n -i -E \"exit-interview\\.json|GameTitle|claude-opus|check-environment|\\.spike\" PLAN.md | head; wc -w PLAN.md","description":"Write and validate exit interview, remove scratch folder, stop my leftovers"}}}
